That, &amp; they were always supposed to be blog posts anyway&hellip; I didn&rsquo;t have a blog then.
A while back I posted very excitedly about an update to the du binary. I forgot to post that I actually used it for something&hellip;
We pushed Druva into production earlier this year for all clients (macOS &amp; Windows). In my opinion Druva is the gold standard for cloud backup.
Before pushing it out, we wanted to know how much data lived in everyone&rsquo;s home folder &amp; the sub-folders. So, of course I made a multi-line Jamf Pro extension attribute&hellip;
#!/bin/sh # macOS version check if [ &#34;$(/usr/bin/sw_vers -productVersion | /usr/bin/awk -F &#39;.&#39; &#39;{print $1}&#39;)&#34; -ge 12 ] then byteblk=&#39;--si&#39; else byteblk=&#39;-h&#39; fi # collect home folder size (collects size of dot files but excludes from display) crntusr=&#34;$(/usr/bin/stat -f %Su /dev/console)&#34; dscldir=&#34;$(/usr/bin/dscl -plist . read /Users/&#34;$crntusr&#34; NFSHomeDirectory)&#34; homedir=&#34;$(/usr/libexec/PlistBuddy -c &#34;print dsAttrTypeStandard\:NFSHomeDirectory:0&#34; /dev/stdin &lt;&lt;&lt; &#34;$dscldir&#34;)&#34; homesiz=&#34;$(/usr/bin/du -d 1 &#34;$byteblk&#34; &#34;$homedir&#34; | /usr/bin/awk &#39;!/\/\./{print}&#39; | /usr/bin/sort -k 2)&#34; echo &#34;&lt;result&gt;$homesiz&lt;/result&gt;&#34; (Why was this necessary? Because &ldquo;Home Folder Size&rdquo; in Settings &gt; Computer Management &gt; Management Framework &gt; Inventory Collection is about as useless as betting against the Harlem Globetrotters&hellip;)
The output in Jamf looks like this:
I then used the Jamf API &amp; a little shell fu to calculate totals (there was some additional screwing around with bc &amp; in spreadsheets after this 😐):
#!/bin/zsh # jamf api apiuser=&#39;&#39; apipswd=&#39;&#39; jamfurl=&#39;&#39; eastrng=&#39;&#39; jamfids() { apidata=&#34;$(/usr/bin/curl -LSs -X GET -H &#39;accept: application/xml&#39; -u &#34;$apiuser:$apipswd&#34; &#34;$jamfurl/$1&#34;)&#34; arrsize=&#34;$(echo &#34;$apidata&#34; | /usr/bin/xmllint --xpath &#34;//size/text()&#34; -)&#34; for ((i=0;i&lt;=$arrsize;i++)) { echo &#34;$apidata&#34; | /usr/bin/xmllint --xpath &#34;concat(//$2[$i]/id/text(),&#39; &#39;)&#34; - } } # get home folder data arr=($(jamfids computers computer)); /bin/sleep 1 echo &#39;Username,Users,Applications,Desktop,Documents,Downloads,Library,Movies,Music,Pictures,Public&#39; &gt; ~/Desktop/sizes.csv for j in &#34;${arr[@]}&#34; do szdat=&#34;$(/usr/bin/curl -LSs -X GET -H &#39;Accept: application/json&#39; -u &#34;$apiuser:$apipswd&#34; &#34;$jamfurl/computers/id/$j&#34; | /opt/homebrew/bin/jq -r &#34;.[] | .extension_attributes[] | select(.name == \&#34;$eastrng\&#34;) | .value&#34;)&#34;; /bin/sleep 1 if [ -z &#34;$szdat&#34; ] then unset szdat; continue else usrnm=&#34;$(/usr/bin/curl -LSs -X GET -H &#39;Accept: application/json&#39; -u &#34;$apiuser:$apipswd&#34; &#34;$jamfurl/computers/id/$j&#34; | /opt/homebrew/bin/jq -r &#39;.computer.general.name&#39;)&#34;; /bin/sleep 1 users=&#34;$(echo &#34;$szdat&#34; | /usr/bin/sed -n &#39;1p&#39; | /usr/bin/awk &#39;{print $1}&#39;)&#34; appls=&#34;$(echo &#34;$szdat&#34; | /usr/bin/awk &#34;/\/Users\/.*\/Applications$/{print \$1}&#34;)&#34; dsktp=&#34;$(echo &#34;$szdat&#34; | /usr/bin/awk &#34;/\/Users\/.*\/Desktop$/{print \$1}&#34;)&#34; dcmnt=&#34;$(echo &#34;$szdat&#34; | /usr/bin/awk &#34;/\/Users\/.*\/Documents$/{print \$1}&#34;)&#34; dwnld=&#34;$(echo &#34;$szdat&#34; | /usr/bin/awk &#34;/\/Users\/.*\/Downloads$/{print \$1}&#34;)&#34; libry=&#34;$(echo &#34;$szdat&#34; | /usr/bin/awk &#34;/\/Users\/.*\/Library$/{print \$1}&#34;)&#34; movie=&#34;$(echo &#34;$szdat&#34; | /usr/bin/awk &#34;/\/Users\/.*\/Movies$/{print \$1}&#34;)&#34; music=&#34;$(echo &#34;$szdat&#34; | /usr/bin/awk &#34;/\/Users\/.*\/Music$/{print \$1}&#34;)&#34; pctrs=&#34;$(echo &#34;$szdat&#34; | /usr/bin/awk &#34;/\/Users\/.*\/Pictures$/{print \$1}&#34;)&#34; publc=&#34;$(echo &#34;$szdat&#34; | /usr/bin/awk &#34;/\/Users\/.*\/Public$/{print \$1}&#34;)&#34; echo &#34;$usrnm,$users,$appls,$dsktp,$dcmnt,$dwnld,$libry,$movie,$music,$pctrs,$publc&#34; &gt;&gt; ~/Desktop/sizes.csv unset appls dcmnt dsktp dwnld libry movie music pctrs publc szdat users usrnm fi done Sample of .csv output:
Enjoy!
